ntent\nThe nested ternary does NOT reveal intent well. `items[0].type === \"sword\" ? 100 : items[0].type === \"amulet\" ? 60 : 0` is confusing — it maps types to base premiums but hides this fact. A lookup table (e.g., a `Map` or object literal) would be much clearer.\n\n**Improvement**: Extract a `BASE_PREMIUM` lookup table mapping item types to base premium amounts. This reveals intent: \"these are the base premiums per item type.\"\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication\nNo duplication yet at 3 items. But the pattern will grow as more types are added (staff, potion, rune) — each `===` check is duplication of intent. A lookup table removes that conceptual duplication.\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\nWe're adding a constant object (+1 constant), but removing a nested ternary (-2 conditionals). Net: fewer conditional elements, better clarity.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Implement Refact"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a9a5a65965434ac996e29719","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"/** Processing fee applied to every quote (in G). */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/**\n * Quote: compute the premium for a list of items for a given customer state.\n * Returns the total premium in G (integer).\n */\nexport function quote(\n  items: Record<string, unknown>[],\n  customerContext: { yearsWithMHPCO: number; contractsSoFar: number }\n): number {\n  if (items.length === 0) return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  const base = items[0].type === \"sword\" ? 100 : items[0].type === \"amulet\" ? 60 : 0;\n  return base + PROCESSING_FEE;\n}","newText":"/** Processing fee applied to every quote (in G). */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Base premium amounts per item type (in G). */\nconst BASE_PREMIUM: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n};\n\n/**\n * Quote: compute the premium for a list of items for a given customer state.\n * Returns the total premium in G (integer).\n */\nexport function quote(\n  items: Record<string, unknown>[],\n  customerConte