ll\n  4. `count % BLOCK_SIZE` — operator\n  5. `count / BLOCK_SIZE` — operator\n  6. `BASE_PREMIUM[ct]` — index lookup\n  7. `BLOCK_SIZE * ...` — multiplication\n  8. `blocks * (basePriceForBlock - BLOCK_PRICE)` — multiplication\n  9. `basePriceForBlock - BLOCK_PRICE` — subtraction  \n  10. `blockDiscount + blocks * ...` — addition assignment (+=)\n  \n  Hmm, let me reconsider. The APP mass model counts invocations as function calls. Let me be more careful:\n  - `??` (nullish coalescing) is an operator, not an invocation\n  - `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%` are operators, count as invocations since they're operations? Or just function calls?\n\n  Actually, in the APP model: invocations = function calls (including operators treated as function calls). Let me count conservatively:\n  \n  1. `new Set(...)` — 1 invocation\n  2. `componentTypes.has(...)` — 1 invocation  \n  3. `??` operators — these are language-level, not function calls. Skip.\n  4. `+` (basePremium += ...) on line 49 — operator\n  5. `+` on line 51\n  6. `%` on line 60\n  7. `/` on line 61\n  8. `*` on line 62\n  9. `-` on line 63\n  10. `*` on line 63\n  \n  The APP model tends to count operators like `+`, `-`, `*` as invocations (mass 2 each). I'll count the key operator invocations:\n  \n  Let me count carefully: each `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`, `??` as an invocation (they're essentially function-like operations):\n  \n  1. `new Set(...)` — invocation (2)\n  2. `+=` on line 49: `basePremium + BASE_PREMIUM[item.type] ?? 0` — the `+` (2) and `??` (2)\n  3. `.has(item.type)` — invocation (2)\n  4. `=` on line 51: `??` (2) and `+` (2)\n  5. `??` on line 59 — (2)\n  6. `%` on line 60 — (2)\n  7. `>` on line 60 — this is a conditional, not invocation. But `>` — hmm, it's a comparison.\n  8. `/` on line 61 — (2)\n  9. `*` on line 62 — (2)\n  10. `??` on line 62 — (2)\n  11. `-` on line 63 — (2)\n  12. `*` on line 63 — (2)\n  13. `+` on line 66 — (2)\n\n  I think I'm overcomplicating this. The APP model is primarily a