 pass: sum base premiums, count component types, and track item-specific surcharges\n  const componentTypes = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\n  const typeCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  let basePremium = 0;\n  let itemSurcharges = 0;\n\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const itemBase = BASE_PREMIUM[item.type] ?? 0;\n    basePremium += itemBase;\n    // Cursed: 50% surcharge on item's base premium\n    if (item.cursed) {\n      itemSurcharges += itemBase * 0.5;\n    }\n    if (componentTypes.has(item.type)) {\n      typeCounts[item.type] = (typeCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Block discount: each block of 3 alike components costs 60 G instead of 3×25=75 G\n  let blockDiscount = 0;\n  for (const ct of componentTypes) {\n    const count = typeCounts[ct] ?? 0;\n    if (count % 3 === 0) {\n      blockDiscount += count * 5;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return basePremium + itemSurcharges - blockDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE;\n}\n\n/**\n * Process a claim against an existing policy.\n * Returns { payout: number, remainingCap: number }.\n */\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: { items: Record<string, unknown>[]; insuranceSum: number },\n  damages: { itemType: string; amount: number }[]\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  throw new Error(\"Not implemented\");\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784950259691},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good. Now let me consider one more refactoring opportunity. The block discount computation loop iterates over `componentTypes` (a Set with 2 members), but there's a subtle issue: if no runes or moonstones are in the items, `typeCounts[ct]` will be undefined, `?? 0` handles it, but we're still iterating the Set. Since the Set only has 2 entries, this is negligible — but for clarity, we could iterate over `typeCounts` keys instead. However, that would be a more complex change. Let me check if there's something more impactful.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the `componentTypes` variable