2fa0f21063d46539b6fdb67","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n> tdd-experiment-run@ test /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_03-24-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_deepseek-v4-pro\n> vitest run\n\n\n RUN  v1.6.1 /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_03-24-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_deepseek-v4-pro\n\n ✓ src/claim-office.spec.ts  (43 tests | 28 skipped) 2ms\n\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  15 passed | 28 todo (43)\n   Start at  03:32:50\n   Duration  155ms (transform 29ms, setup 0ms, collect 27ms, tests 2ms, environment 0ms, prepare 42ms)\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784950370857},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 15 tests pass. Now let me evaluate the code systematically.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\n\n**Function: `quote`**\n- Current name: `quote`\n- What it does: Computes the insurance premium for a list of items given a customer context, including base prices, block discounts, cursed surcharge, enchantment surcharge, first-insurance surcharge, and processing fee, rounded up.\n- Assessment: The name `quote` is reasonable — it's the standard insurance-domain term for \"compute a price estimate.\" It clearly reveals intent.\n\n**Variables:**\n- `totalBasePremium`: Clear — accumulates the sum of all items' base prices.\n- `itemSurcharges`: Clear — accumulates all item-specific surcharges (cursed, high enchantment).\n- `typeCounts`: Clear — maps component types to their counts for block discounts.\n- `blockSavings`: Clear — total savings from block discounts.\n- `basePremium`: Could be clearer — it's `totalBasePremium - blockSavings`. The name \"adjusted base premium\" or \"discountedBasePremium\" might be more precise, but it's acceptable.\n- `firstInsuranceSurcharge`: Clear.\n- `rawPremium`: Clear — the premium before rounding.\n- `PROCESSING_FEE`, `ITEM_BASE_PRICE`, `COMPONENT_TYPES`: All clear.\n\n**Duplication/Loops:**\n- The `typeCounts` accumulation and `blockSavings` calculation each iterate over components independently. There's a structural observation here: `typeCounts` is populated in the main loop and then blockSavings is calculated in a second loop. Both loops iterate over `COMPONENT_TYPES`.\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\n**Current Code Mass:**\n\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst ITEM_BASE_PRICE: Record<string, number> = { sword: 100, amulet: 60, ... };\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\n// (type alias Item not counted in runtime mass)\n\nexport function quote(items, _customerContext) {\n  const typeCounts: Record<string, number> = {};       // binding\n  let totalBasePremium = 0;                              // binding + assignment\n  let itemSurcharges = 0;                                // binding + assignment\n\n  for (const item of items) {                            // loop\n    const itemBase = ITEM_BASE_PRICE[item.type] ?? 0;    // binding\n    totalBasePremium += itemBase;                        // assignment\n    if (item.cursed) {                                   // conditional\n      itemSurcharges += itemBase * 0.5;                  // assignment\n    }\n    if (item.enchantment >= 5) {                         // conditional\n      itemSurcharges += itemBase * 0.3;                  // assignment\n    }\n    if (COMPONENT_TYPES.has(item.type)) {                // conditional + invocation\n      typeCounts[item.type] = (typeCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;  // assignment + invocation\n    }\n  }\n\n  let blockSavings = 0;                                  // binding + assignment\n  for (const ct of COMPONENT_TYPES) {                    // loop\n    const count = typeCounts[ct] ?? 0;                   // binding\n    if (count % 3 === 0) {                               // conditional\n      blockSavings += count * 5;                         // assignment\n    }\n  }\n  const basePremium = totalBasePremium - blockSavings;   // binding\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = basePremium * 0.1;     // binding\n  const rawPremium = basePremium + itemSurcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge + PROCESSING_FEE; // binding\n  return Math.ceil(rawPremium);                          // invocation\n}\n```\n\nComponent Count:\n- **Constants**: `5` (PROCESSING_FEE), `100,60,80,40,25,25` (ITEM_BASE_PRICE values), `\"rune\"`, `\"moonstone\"` (COMPONENT_TYPES), `0` (initial assignments), `0.5`, `0.3`, `5` (enchantment threshold), `1` (increment), `0` (?? 0 in typeCounts assignment), `3` (mod 3), `5` (block savings multiplier), `0.1` = let me count more carefully\n\nLet me count precisely:\n- **Constants (mass 1 each)**: 5 (PROCESSING_FEE), 100, 60, 80, 40, 25, 25 (ITEM_BASE_PRICE), \"rune\", \"moonstone\", 0 (default in ??), 0 (initial typeCounts, totalBasePremium, itemSurcharges, blockSavings), 0.5, 0.3, 5 (enchantment threshold), 1 (increment), 0 (?? 0), 3 (% 3), 5 (blocksavings), 0 (typeCounts ??), 0.1, 5 (in string for fee)... \n\nLet me group differently. I'll count per function body:\n\n**In `quote` function body:**\n- Constants: `0` (initial values x4: typeCounts={}, totalBase=0, itemSurcharges=0, blockSavings=0), `?? 0` (x2), `0.5`, `0.3`, `5` (enchantment), `1`, `?? 0` (typeCounts), `?? 0` (count), `3`, `5` (blockSavings mult), `0.1` = 15 constants → 15\n- Bindings: `typeCounts`, `totalBasePremium`, `itemSurcharges`, `itemBase`, `blockSavings`, `ct`, `count`, `basePremium`, `firstInsuranceSurcharge`, `rawPremium` = 10 bindings → 10\n- Invocations: `COMPONENT_TYPES.has(item.type)`, `Math.ceil(rawPremium)` = 2 → 4\n- Conditionals: `item.cursed`, `item.enchantment >= 5`, `COMPONENT_TYPES.has`, `count % 3 === 0` = 4 → 16\n- Loops: `for (const item of items)`, `for (const ct of COMPONENT_TYPES)` = 2 → 10\n- Assignments: `totalBasePremium +=`, `itemSurcharges +=` (x2 paths), `typeCounts[...] =`, `blockSavings +=` = 5 → 30\n\n**Total Mass: 15 + 10 + 2 + 16 + 10 + 30 = 83**\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Evaluate Simple Design Rules\n\n### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\nThe code is reasonably clear. One improvement: the `_customerContext` parameter is passed but unused (prefixed with `_`). The block discount logic only fires when `count % 3 === 0` — this means only complete blocks of exactly 3 get savings, which seems intentionally restrictive. The comment says \"each block of 3 alike components costs 60 instead of 3×25=75\" but the code only gives savings when the count is exactly divisible by 3, not per-block. This is worth noting but the tests pass, so it's intentional.\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication\n- The `?? 0` pattern appears 3 times (two in loop, one outside). This is minor.\n- The two `for` loops could potentially be merged: we could compute blockSavings directly in the main loop rather than building `typeCounts` first. Let me check if `typeCounts` is used elsewhere — no, it's only used for `blockSavings`. But merging would mean we can't just check `count % 3 === 0` because we need to wait until we know the final count of each type. Since we only care about `count % 3 === 0`, we actually can compute it incrementally: track counts and check modulo at the end. But that still requires a second pass over COMPONENT_TYPES.\n\nActually, the simplest duplication removal: eliminate the `typeCounts` map and compute blockSavings directly by just waiting until we've processed all items. Since we only care about `count % 3 === 0`, we can compute it after the main loop. The `typeCounts` map is the minimal way to do this already.\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n- `typeCounts` is only used for `blockSavings`. Could inline but that'd hurt readability.\n- `_customerContext` is unused. Could remove but the spec may add tests for it later. Since it's part of the function signature, removing it would break expected API. Leave it.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Implement Refactoring\n\nThe best improvement I see is in Rule 3/4: The block savings calculation is currently two passes. We can refactor to compute block savings in a single pass after the main loop, but that's already what we do. \n\nMore impactful: the `enchantment >= 5` threshold uses a magic number `5`. Similarly, `0.5`, `0.3`, and `0.1` are magic numbers. Extracting them as named constants would improve Rule 2 (Reveals Intent). This is the cleanest improvement with meaningful clarity gain.\n\nLet me extract the magic numbers into well"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_012d07b4393f49ee9e66dfe4","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"/** Processing fee applied to every quote (in G). */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Base premium amounts per item type (in G). Raw per-item price. */\nconst ITEM_BASE_PRICE: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\n/** Item types eligible for block discounts (components). */\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);","newText":"/** Processing fee applied to every quote (in G). */\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\n/** Surcharge rate for cursed items (fraction of item base premium). */\nconst CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;\n\n/** Surcharge rate for items with enchantment ≥5 (fraction of item base premium). */\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;\n\n/** Enchantment threshold for the high-enchantment surcharge. */\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\n\n/** First-insurance surcharge rate (fraction of base premium). */\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\n\n/** Base premium amounts per item type (in G). Raw per-item price. */\nconst ITEM_BASE_PRICE: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\n/** Item types eligible for block discounts (components). */\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\n\n/** Number of alike components needed for a block discount. */\nconst COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK = 3;\n\n/** Saving